      "claim": "An increase in aggregate demand shifts the AD curve rightward.",
      "evidence": "By definition, a rise in aggregate demand is represented by a rightward shift of the AD curve in the AD‑AS model.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Moving rightward along an upward‑sloping SRAS raises the equilibrium price level.",
      "evidence": "With a positively sloped SRAS, a higher quantity of output is supplied only at a higher price; graphically the new equilibrium after a rightward AD shift lies at a higher P.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Moving rightward along an upward‑sloping SRAS also raises equilibrium real GDP.",
      "evidence": "Because the SRAS is not horizontal, the rightward AD shift leads to a larger quantity of output; algebraically, SRAS can be written Y = Ȳ + α(P – P_e) with α>0, so a higher P yields a higher Y.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
